docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:9:A personal investment operating system for a Schwab taxable brokerage account. A Rails application acts as the system of record and the sole gateway to Schwab's Trader API. Claude acts as the analysis and workflow agent on top of it, via an MCP server the Rails app exposes. The human approves every order.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:15:- Positions, balances, orders, and transactions synced from Schwab automatically. No hand-maintained portfolio.csv.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:17:- A guardrail engine (risk rules as data, not vibes) that evaluates every proposed order before it can reach Schwab.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:23:- Fully autonomous trading. No order reaches Schwab without explicit human approval.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:36:      │                              │  - Schwab API client      │
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:37:      │ reads                        │  - Sync jobs (Solid Queue)│
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:51:**Why an MCP server and not the agent hitting Schwab directly:** one credential holder, one audit log, one place guardrails run. The MCP tool surface is the contract.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:55:### 5.1 Schwab Integration (Rails)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:57:- **App registration:** Schwab Developer Portal, Trader API - Individual product. Manual approval, 1-3 business days. Callback URL must be HTTPS and match exactly.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:63:- **Client generation:** the api-evangelist OpenAPI specs turned out to be skeletal (minimal order schema, no response schemas for chains), so treat them as scaffolding only. Better references for the real shapes: the schwab-py order-builder docs and community client libraries that model the full schema; replace with Schwab's official schemas once portal access is approved. Wrap in a thin service layer with retry/backoff and rate-limit awareness.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:65:- **Order submission idempotency:** the Schwab order POST has no client-supplied order id (per community clients; confirm post-approval), so dedupe is local. The approved → submitted transition runs under a row lock and is the only code path that can call the order endpoint. On an ambiguous outcome (timeout, 5xx, connection drop after send), the proposal enters `submission_unknown`; a reconciliation job queries the orders endpoint for a matching order (instrument, qty, side, price, time window) before any retry. No blind retries, ever.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:75:- `trades` (journal). A trade is one instrument's position lifecycle from zero back to zero; scale-ins and scale-outs are `trade_legs` linked to transactions. Fields: setup tag, thesis, planned stop/target, realized R-multiple, post_mortem. Fills with no originating proposal (orders placed at Schwab directly) create a trade in `incomplete` state with no planned stop; the EOD brief asks for the missing stop/setup rather than computing a bogus R.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:86:- Market data (quotes for holdings + watchlist): on demand from agent tools, cached 1-5 min. Schwab Market Data API is primary; FMP optional secondary for fundamentals/screening not covered by Schwab.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:87:- **Market calendar:** "market hours" everywhere in this doc resolves through Schwab's market-hours endpoint (holidays, early closes), cached daily. Job schedules and blackout windows derive from it; nothing hardcodes 9:30-16:00.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:108:Purpose: replace gut-feel stop placement with a computed acceptable range ("collar") derived from several standard methods, evaluated against price history from Schwab Market Data (FMP fallback).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:130:#### 5.4.2 Stop Order Placement (decided: real stops at Schwab)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:132:An app-only stop with 5-15 minute polling is not protection. Every approved entry is submitted as a bracket: the entry order with a one-triggers-another child stop order (Schwab order strategies support this; confirm exact strategy type names post-approval). Consequences:
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:137:- Working stops are reconciled against Schwab on every orders sync; a stop that disappears at the broker (cancelled out-of-band) flags the position `unprotected`.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:148:Write tools (all create records; none touch Schwab directly):
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:159:- **Interactive (human present):** the Claude app (desktop/Cowork/web) with the Rails MCP server registered as a remote connector. Rails exposes MCP over HTTPS with bearer-token auth (token in dokku config, rotatable). This is where ad hoc analysis, screening, and order proposals happen, billed through the Claude subscription seat.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:160:- **Scheduled (unattended):** Solid Queue jobs on the VPS call the Anthropic Messages API directly with an API key, passing tool definitions that map to the same internal service layer the MCP server uses (in-process, no MCP hop). Output is rendered to email. This covers the 07:00 brief, 16:30 EOD, and Sunday review. **Unattended runs get read-only tools only.** These runs ingest untrusted text (transcripts, news, fetched pages), so `propose_order`, `add_watchlist_item`, and `annotate_trade` are not in their tool set; anything a brief wants to suggest is rendered as text in the email, and the human creates the proposal interactively. Write tools exist only in the interactive runtime. Each scheduled job also has a hard cap on tool turns. Rationale: unattended runs can't depend on a desktop app being open, and keeping the scheduler in Rails means the VPS is the single ops surface (one place for cron, retries, failure alerts, and the token-health gate).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:178:- Sunday edge review: per-setup expectancy over trailing sample, flag negative-expectancy setups, propose deactivation (human confirms).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:197:- **Notifications, revised:** time-sensitive alerts move from email to push once the app exists: new proposal, fill/cancel, halt tripped, `unprotected` position, token expiring. Briefs and the Sunday ritual stay email (long-form, read on a desktop anyway). Push payloads carry no financial figures; they deep-link and the app fetches.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:202:### 5.9 Deployment (decided)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:204:- Host: Meticulous Greencloud VPS, Ubuntu 24, deployed via dokku (matches existing Meticulous infra).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:205:- HTTPS via dokku letsencrypt plugin; the app's OAuth callback URL is a stable route on the app's domain, registered exactly in the Schwab app config.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:206:- Postgres and Solid Queue as dokku services; daily DB dump to offsite storage.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:207:- Schwab app key/secret in dokku config vars; tokens encrypted at rest in Postgres.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:208:- The Sunday re-auth (see 5.10) happens from any browser; only the callback needs to reach the VPS.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:209:- **Shadow mode (Schwab has no sandbox):** a global flag, on by default in production until deliberately turned off. In shadow mode the entire pipeline runs, including human approval, but the submission step writes a `would_have_submitted` audit event and the order payload instead of calling Schwab. Playwright runs in shadow mode. Phase 2 ships with shadow mode on; it is turned off only after a stated number of shadowed approvals reconcile cleanly against what would have been sent. Toggling it is an audit event.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:211:### 5.10 Weekly Ritual (decided: Sundays, email)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:215:- Sunday evening email bundles: (a) the weekly edge review, (b) a re-auth link to redo the Schwab browser login, (c) system health summary (last sync times, job failures, token age).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:216:- Re-authing every Sunday resets the 7-day clock so it never expires mid-week. Caveat: expiry is 7 days from issuance, so the email lands earlier than the prior week's auth time, and a daily health check escalates (Mon, then Tue) if the re-auth hasn't happened. On expiry, sync jobs pause and queue rather than erroring.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:218:### 5.11 Testing & CI (mirrors Talos / Patchvault conventions)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:224:  4. Schwab client service layer: VCR/WebMock cassettes only. The suite never touches the live API; cassettes get scrubbed of tokens and encrypted account values before commit.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:226:- **Playwright** for clickable UI: approval queue flow (approve/reject a proposal, override a stop outside the collar with a logged reason), dashboard renders with seeded data, risk-rule editor. These run against a seeded test DB with a fake Schwab adapter, not cassettes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:232:A second rule set alongside the trading guardrails, covering the book-level behaviors from post-crash market research (concentration of best days, lump-sum vs staged deployment, threshold rebalancing, correlation breakdown, leveraged-proxy discipline). Kept structurally separate from per-trade guardrails: trading rules judge one order, allocation rules judge the whole book. Both feed the same proposal → approval pipeline; nothing here auto-executes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:234:**Data model additions:** `allocation_targets` (asset or class, target weight, drift band), `instrument_tags` (leveraged/amplified flag per symbol), `deployment_policies` (per cash-inflow handling).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:239:2. **Leveraged-proxy exception.** Symbols tagged leveraged/amplified are excluded from mechanical rebalance proposals. Drift on those instead triggers a thesis-review task: the proposal renders the original thesis from the journal/watchlist next to current numbers, and the human decides deploy / hold / exit. Encodes "the discipline is the same, the decision is not mechanical."
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:240:3. **Deployment policy for new cash.** Cash inflows (detected via transactions sync) trigger a deployment proposal per the policy John sets in `deployment_policies` (immediate, staged over N weeks, or ask-each-time). The proposal renders the relevant base rates (lump-sum vs staged historical win rates) as context; the policy itself is John's call, stored as data, and changeable only in the rule editor.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:241:4. **Cash-drag diagnostic.** The Sunday review computes time-in-cash vs the deployment policy and flags divergence ("policy says deployed; book is 40% cash for 3 weeks"). Informational, never a forced action.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:242:5. **Correlation stress check.** Weekly job computes rolling 60-day correlations between each holding (and tagged "diversifier" instruments) and SPY from price history. When a diversifier's correlation exceeds a threshold (default 0.6), the Sunday review flags that the diversification assumption is currently failing. Diagnostic only.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:243:6. **Flow/sentiment input.** Weekly fund-flow datapoint (ICI weekly long-term fund flows; source via FMP if available, else fetched from ICI's published release) rendered in the Sunday review as context alongside the edge stats. Data in, judgment human.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:245:**Boundary:** the module surfaces mechanics and base rates; it does not embed a market view. Which patterns become policy (rebalance bands, deployment policy, what counts as a diversifier) is authored by John in the rule editor and versioned like every other rule.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:247:**UI:** allocation targets + drift live on the dashboard below positions; rebalance and deployment proposals use the standard Flow-2 proposal detail; thesis-review proposals add the original-thesis panel. Rule authoring goes in the (desktop) rule editor.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:251:**Phase 1 (read-only):** Schwab OAuth + token lifecycle, account/position/transaction sync, dashboard, journal auto-population from fills, memory repo, MCP read tools, daily briefs.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:253:**Phase 2.5 (allocation module):** targets, drift checks, rebalance/deployment proposals, correlation and cash-drag diagnostics in the Sunday review (§5.12).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:259:- Hosting: Meticulous Greencloud VPS, Ubuntu 24 + dokku (see 5.9).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:260:- Notifications: email only for now; Sunday ritual bundles re-auth + edge review (see 5.10).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:261:- FMP: kept as secondary connector (fundamentals, screeners, anything Schwab market data lacks).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:267:- Greeks: Schwab option chains and option quotes include delta, gamma, theta, vega, rho, plus implied volatility, theoretical value, and time value. No local greeks computation needed for the eventual options module.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:269:- Review v8 (Sep 1): real bracket stops at Schwab; local idempotency with reconcile-before-retry; unattended runtime is read-only; trades defined as zero-to-zero lifecycles keyed by CUSIP; shadow mode on by default; broker balances as settled-cash source; market calendar from Schwab; flow-adjusted equity series; min rebalance notional; single-writer memory repo; one append-only audit table.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:275:**Data (resolved):** Schwab Market Data API provides option chains with greeks (delta, gamma, theta, vega, rho), implied volatility, theoretical value, and time value per contract. No local computation needed. Option symbols use OSI format (e.g., `AAPL  250117C00150000`), which touches the symbol handling everywhere: positions sync, journal linking, quotes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:290:## 9. Open Questions (remaining)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:294:## 10. Risks
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:297:- Schwab individual-tier API terms: this is fine for personal use; anything multi-user requires their commercial review.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:298:- Order idempotency: no client order id on the Schwab side, so dedupe is local (row-locked single submission path, `submission_unknown` state, reconcile-before-retry; see 5.1). Duplicate submission is the one bug this system cannot afford.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:5:Sections: decisions, migrations, jobs, tool surface, journal builder, stops and open risk, snapshots, UI, testing, deploy, build order, deferred items, Schwab assumptions, working method.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:11:1. **Secrets in Rails credentials**, production-only file (`config/credentials/production.yml.enc`), keys `schwab.app_key`, `schwab.app_secret`, `mcp.bearer_token`, `anthropic.api_key`, `resend.api_key`, plus Active Record Encryption keys. `SCHWAB_CALLBACK_URL` and `MEMORY_REPO_PATH` stay in dokku ENV (not secret). The dokku `SCHWAB_APP_KEY`/`SCHWAB_APP_SECRET` vars get unset once the credentials file is on the server. Rotating the MCP token is an edit to the credentials file plus a deploy.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:14:4. **Email via Resend over SMTP**, no gem; API key in credentials. Production login and re-auth depend on it, so Resend DNS is a prerequisite for the first production deploy.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:30:| 3 | `accounts` | `schwab_hash` (Schwab's opaque hashValue, unique), `account_type` enum (taxable/ira, nullable: set by the operator since Schwab only reports CASH or MARGIN), `trading_enabled` bool, `nickname`, `last_synced_at`, `raw` jsonb. A plaintext account number is never a column. | yes |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:31:| 4 | `instruments` | `cusip` (nullable, unique where present), `schwab_instrument_id` (nullable, unique where present), `symbol`, `symbol_history` jsonb `[{symbol, until}]` (UTC), `asset_type`, `leveraged` bool default false, `description`, `predecessor_instrument_id` (self FK, set on CUSIP succession: reverse split, merger, spin-off). Identity resolution order: CUSIP, then Schwab instrument id, then symbol+asset_type as last resort with a `needs_review` audit. | yes |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:37:| 10 | `activities` (the PRD's `transactions`; renamed because `transaction` is reserved by Active Record) | One row per Schwab activity. `account_id`, `instrument_id` (nullable for cash-only), `order_id` (FK, nullable), `broker_order_id` (nullable), `broker_activity_id` (unique per account), `kind` enum (fill, sweep, dividend, interest, fee, deposit, withdrawal, transfer, corporate_action, other), `status` (Schwab's VALID/INVALID/PENDING; only VALID is journaled), `sub_type` string (raw Schwab type), `side`, `quantity`, `price`, `commission`, `fees` (summed from the activity's fee transfer items), `net_amount`, `trade_date`, `settlement_date`, `executed_at`, `raw` jsonb, `journaled_at` | yes |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:45:| 18 | `sync_runs` | `feed` enum (accounts, positions, orders, activities, market_hours, memory_repo, snapshots; balances ride with positions), `account_id` (nullable; cursors are per account and feed), `status` (running, succeeded, partial, failed, skipped_token), `started_at`, `finished_at`, `error`, `records_touched`, `unmapped_values` jsonb, `window_from`, `window_to` (captured before the request; the next window starts from it), `gap_from` (set when the computed window start is later than the last success: data Schwab can no longer return) | yes |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:50:Solid Queue / Cache / Cable schemas already generated. Every datetime column is `timestamptz` (adapter default set in an initializer). Schema format is `structure.sql` for the audit triggers; the dump pins Postgres 17 settings, so dokku runs the postgres:17 image. Active Record Encryption: production keys live in `config/credentials/production.yml.enc`; development and test share `config/credentials.yml.enc` (dev keys) with fixed test keys in `config/environments/test.rb`.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:56:## 2. Jobs (Solid Queue, `config/recurring.yml`, all times America/New_York)
docs/phase-1-plan.md:58:**Token model (revised after the step 4 counselors review).** Pause state is *derived* from the current token, never written by a failure. `schwab_tokens` gains `revoked_at`; `SchwabToken.current` is the newest by `issued_at, id`; `usable?` = present, not refresh-expired (`issued_at + 168.hours`, elapsed time not calendar days), not revoked. `SchwabToken.issue!(response)` inserts the new row and clears `system_settings.sync_paused_reason` in one transaction, enqueuing `Sync::CatchUpJob` after commit. `SchwabToken#refresh!` takes the row lock, reloads, and returns early if the row is no longer current or was refreshed in the last minute (so two workers cannot double-refresh and a stale row cannot act); it builds the client inside the lock; `access_expires_at` = now + `expires_in` − 60 s; `refresh_token` = response value or the existing one; `refresh_expires_at` is never touched. On `ReauthRequired` it sets `revoked_at` on the current row only, records `last_refresh_error` outside the lock so a rollback cannot lose it, audits `token_refresh_failed`, and sets `sync_paused_reason`. On `ServerError`/`TransportError` it records the error and does nothing else. `ensure_fresh!(margin:)` refreshes proactively when the access token is near expiry, so a 401 is the safety net rather than the norm. `Schwab.client` raises `Schwab::NoUsableToken` rather than letting nil reach a request.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:60:**Token gating.** Only `Sync::TickJob` and the agent jobs are scheduled; everything else is enqueued by them. When `SchwabToken.current` is not usable, the tick job writes one `sync_runs` `skipped_token` row per hour, audits `sync_paused_token_expired` once per episode, and enqueues nothing. No self-re-enqueue, so the queue cannot grow. The re-auth callback (`issue!`) clears the pause, audits `sync_resumed`, and enqueues `Sync::CatchUpJob`, which runs all feeds with windows widened to cover the gap (capped at the 60-day limit). Agent jobs run degraded rather than wait: `degraded: true`, the prompt states the token is unusable and every number carries its as-of time, the email subject is prefixed, and the token status line sits at the top of every brief so a dead nag path is still visible daily. Snapshots are not written for days without a fresh balance.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:62:**Re-auth flow.** `SchwabController#authorize` (login required) stores a random `state` with a timestamp in the session and redirects to the authorize URL. `#callback` (login required; an unauthenticated hit is rejected without storing a return-to URL, so the code never lands in the session) reads and deletes the pending state; no pending state renders "nothing pending, start again" with no audit; a mismatch audits `re_auth_failed` as CSRF; `params[:error]` (user cancelled) renders the failure without calling the token endpoint. On success: `exchange_code`, then `/accounts/accountNumbers` must overlap the known `accounts` hashes (first auth accepts anything) or the token is not made current and `re_auth_failed` is audited; then `issue!`, audit `re_auth_succeeded`, and **redirect** to a clean success page so a browser refresh cannot resubmit the single-use code. A failed exchange never writes to `schwab_tokens`. The retry link goes to `authorize`. `code`, `state`, and `session` join `filter_parameters`. The callback URL comes only from `SCHWAB_CALLBACK_URL`, never from the request. `fake_authorize` 404s unless the adapter is fake and the environment is not production, and `?fail=1` redirects with a bad code for Playwright.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:64:**Health and nags.** `Schwab::TokenHealthJob` runs 08:00 and 20:00 ET. Nag when: no usable token (absent, revoked, or expired), sent at every run; or the 24-hour warning, sent once per token row (deduped on token id in the audit data). Monday/Tuesday escalation fires when `SchwabToken.current.issued_at` is before the most recent Sunday 18:00 ET, which is the PRD's literal "the Sunday re-auth hasn't happened"; no buffer, no comparison against the coming Sunday. Cron strings carry `America/New_York` explicitly. The refresh task gets a higher queue priority than sync.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:68:| `Schwab::RefreshAccessTokenJob` | every 25 min, high priority | `SchwabToken.current&.refresh!`; no-op when there is no usable token |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:69:| `Schwab::TokenHealthJob` | daily 08:00 + 20:00 | See "Health and nags" above. Audits `token_nag_sent` with the token id and reason. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:70:| `Sync::TickJob` | every 5 min | Sole scheduled sync entry point. Refreshes `market_days` for today (New York) plus 7 days when missing or `source: fallback` (one call per date; a market-hours failure never aborts the tick). Due checks compare against the last *started* run per account and feed with 1-minute slack, and a `running` run is never due; jobs also declare Solid Queue `limits_concurrency` keyed on account and feed so backlog cannot double-enqueue. Cadences apply to trading-enabled accounts: positions (15 min inside the session window pre-market through post-market, 60 min otherwise), orders and activities (5 min inside, 60 outside); read-only accounts sync hourly. `Snapshots::DailyJob` once per market day after `regular_closes_at` + 20 min, only when that day's calendar row came from Schwab. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:80:| `Jobs::FailureDigestJob` | daily 17:15 (after the EOD) | One email for a fixed 24-hour window: failed and partial `sync_runs` by feed (token-skipped rows are the nag's), failed and capped `agent_runs`, runs still `running` past 20 minutes (swept to failed first), finished briefs never emailed, and Solid Queue failed executions by job class; nothing sent when empty |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:82:| `Agent::MorningBriefJob` | 07:03 weekdays (after the 07:00 tick has enqueued the syncs); the calendar row decides | See §3 |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:84:| `Agent::SundayReviewJob` | Sunday 18:00 | Phase 1 stub, no API call: records a `sunday_review` run with no tokens and sends the re-auth link, token age, feeds, failures, and the stored setup stats |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:86:**Sync runs.** `SyncRun.record(feed, account:, window:)` wraps every feed: creates the row, captures `window_to` before the request, rescues `NoUsableToken`, `AuthError`, and `ReauthRequired` into `skipped_token`, rescues other `Schwab::Error` into `failed`, counts quarantined records into `partial` with `unmapped_values` (raw type, description, subAccount, sign, item shape), and stamps `records_touched`. `partial` advances the cursor; `failed` does not. A cash-affecting `other` activity makes the run `partial`, never a quiet success.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:88:**Instrument identity** (`Instrument.resolve(ref)`): CUSIP, then Schwab instrument id, then symbol plus asset type **matching only rows with no CUSIP**, then an `instrument_by_symbol` lookup, then create a provisional row. A weaker-key match backfills a missing CUSIP or instrument id from the ref but never overwrites a differing one. A CUSIP match with a different symbol calls `rename!`. The `instrument_identity_fallback` audit fires once, when a provisional row is created, not on every resolve. `RecordNotUnique` is handled with a re-lookup because positions and activities race.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:92:**Market calendar.** `MarketDay.for(date)` reads the row or fetches it (source schwab). When the token is unusable or the call fails it writes a weekday-open 9:30 to 16:00 row with `source: fallback`, which drives cadence only: snapshots and market-day briefs require a Schwab-sourced row, and fallback rows are re-fetched on every tick until they flip. "Today" is computed in America/New_York.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:94:**Schwab client:** built to `docs/schwab-api-contract.md`. Net::HTTP; plaintext account numbers scrubbed inside the client before any body is returned; logs carry path templates (`/accounts/{hash}/orders`), never hashes, bodies, or headers; one time helper for the three input and three output formats; `BigDecimal` for every money and quantity value; a shared Solid Cache rate budget of 100 requests/minute across Puma and the worker; retries on 429, 5xx, and transport errors for GETs and the refresh grant only (1, 4, 16 s with jitter, `Retry-After` capped at 60 s), never on 4xx; one refresh-and-retry on a 401. Error classes: `AuthError`, `ReauthRequired`, `RateLimited`, `ServerError`, `RequestError`, `TransportError`, `UnknownOutcome` (non-idempotent calls only, chosen by HTTP method), `MappingError`. Interface adds `order(hash, order_id)` so working stops older than the lookback are re-fetched by id, and `instrument(symbol_or_cusip)` for identity fallback. Mappers return `Data` structs with a scrubbed `raw` member; jobs never see a Schwab field name. `Schwab::FakeClient` sits below the mappers and returns raw-shaped hashes with a scriptable per-call queue. Sync runs can finish `partial` with an `unmapped_values` count when individual records are quarantined.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:106:| `get_quotes(symbols)` | Schwab `/marketdata/v1/quotes`, cached 2 min in Solid Cache, each quote stamped with fetched_at; when the token is expired returns the cached value with `stale: true` or an explicit unavailable error |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:107:| `get_price_history(symbol, range)` | Schwab `/marketdata/v1/pricehistory`, cached 5 min |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:114:**MCP server (revised after the step 8 counselors review).** `McpController < ActionController::API` at `match "/mcp", via: [post, get, delete]`: a `before_action` checks the bearer (`Bearer` scheme only, case-insensitive; `secure_compare`; never logged) and answers 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"` and a one-line JSON body, no HTML page and no `resource_metadata` (that would send Claude looking for an authorization server that does not exist). The action builds `MCP::Server` from the registry and hands the request to the gem's `StreamableHTTPTransport` with `stateless: true`, `enable_json_response: true`, `serve_subscriptions_listen: false`, `dns_rebinding_protection: false` (Rails host authorization owns the Host check; feeding the gem `request.host` would have it match the header against itself), and `max_request_bytes` well under the 4 MiB default (Rails parses the JSON body for its log line before the gem's cap runs, so the nginx `client_max_body_size` in §9 is the real first guard); the transport handles GET, DELETE, the protocol-version header, notifications (202), and both the 2025 handshake and the 2026-07-28 stateless transport. Rails `rate_limit to: 60, within: 1.minute` on the controller; JSON only; no CORS. The server advertises only the `tools` capability. Tools return `MCP::Tool::Response` with `structured_content` plus a text block of the same JSON, `additionalProperties: false` and `required` on every input and output schema, `validate_tool_call_results` on outside production so schema drift fails a spec rather than a Claude session, a failed call carries `retry_after` in `_meta`, and annotations `read_only_hint: true, destructive_hint: false, idempotent_hint: true, open_world_hint: false`. Tool exceptions never reach the transport: one dispatch wrapper rescues, writes the `tool_calls` row in an `ensure`, and returns a tool error carrying only the Schwab error message. Quotes and price history are cached with a long expiry and their own `fetched_at`; age beyond the freshness window triggers a refetch and a failed refetch returns the old entry with `stale: true`; the tools draw on a 70/min ceiling of the shared Schwab budget (passed as `budget:` to `Schwab.client`, taken once per request inside the client) so a chatty session cannot starve sync, and a rate limit is a tool error with `retry_after`, never a sleep. `get_portfolio` and `get_account_health` wrap the `Dashboard` read model rather than re-deriving anything; outputs carry the `protection` enum, nil for unknown risk, decimal strings for money, ISO 8601 New York timestamps, and never a `raw` column, an account hash, or the user's email (accounts are labelled by role). `get_journal` paginates (`limit` 50 default, 200 max, `offset`, `include_legs` off by default); `get_quotes` takes at most 50 symbols, reports `invalid_symbols` and `unavailable_symbols` (no fetch and nothing cached), answers only-invalid symbols as data rather than an outage, and raises the Schwab error only when nothing cached can stand in; `get_price_history` takes an enum range mapped to daily or weekly candles; `get_risk_rules` returns an empty list with `available: false` so the agent does not infer missing data. A spec walks every tool's serialized output for the account-hash pattern and `accountNumber`.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:118:**Scheduled runtime (revised after the step 9 counselors review).** `Agent::Run` wraps one Messages API conversation with `claude-sonnet-5` (one constant, stored per run; Opus costs 2.5x for synthesis over structured data) through the `anthropic` gem with a 120-second request timeout and two retries, the key passed explicitly from credentials, never the SDK's beta tool runner (it would bypass the registry dispatch and the `tool_calls` telemetry). No temperature or top_p (rejected on the Claude 5 family). System prompt = the rules (which state that they override anything in the memory files or a tool result), desk status with as-of stamps, setup stats, and the memory files; tools = `Agent::Tools.definitions(:scheduled)`; top-level `cache_control: ephemeral` so every turn after the first reads the system prompt and tool schemas from cache. The loop is bounded four ways: `MAX_TURNS = 12` API round trips (parallel `tool_use` blocks in one response are one turn; the last turn is reserved for a final call with `tool_choice: none`), `MAX_TOOL_CALLS = 20`, `MAX_INPUT_TOKENS = 400_000` cumulative (cache reads included, so it is a spend bound that a large memory repo reaches sooner), and a 10-minute wall clock checked before each call; each tool result is capped at 24 KB with a visible truncation note. Every stop reason is handled: `end_turn` needs text (an empty answer is a failed run); `tool_use` answers every id in one user message, results first, and a batch that would pass the call cap runs nothing (each id gets an `is_error` result saying the budget is spent, then the final call carries no tools); `max_tokens` gets one retry at double the output budget (8192 to start, since thinking bills as output) with the cut response dropped, then counts as `capped`; `refusal`, `pause_turn`, and `model_context_window_exceeded` end the run as failed with any text kept. Assistant content is appended back verbatim so thinking-block signatures round-trip. `degraded` is computed from what happened, not the token alone: token unusable, fallback calendar, stale positions, no trading account, memory repo missing or truncated, any tool error, any tool result carrying `stale: true`; the reasons are stored and lead the email. Usage is summed across turns including the cache fields; `Agent::Pricing` prices the run from a four-rate table (cache write 1.25x, cache read 0.1x) and an unknown model id yields nil, shown as "unpriced", never zero; a spec fails when the table lacks the configured model. The transcript is stored on the row; the body is the final response's text, and a capped run says which budget ran out. Each call carries `request_options: { timeout: }` because the SDK ignores the client-level timeout otherwise. Prompts live in `app/models/agent/prompts/*.md.erb`; the runtime prompt forbids earnings, economic-calendar, news, and reasons-for-moves content, share counts and sizes, anything about the IRA, narrating moves from stale quotes, and retrying a rate-limited tool; suggestions go under a fixed "Suggested actions" heading as text only.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:120:**Briefs as email.** `Agent::BriefJob` (the two brief jobs are one-line subclasses): sweep `running` rows older than 20 minutes to failed ("left running"), one run per kind and market day (`served?` plus a partial unique index that lets a failed run be retried), a `skipped` row on a closed day, run whenever the calendar row says trading regardless of source (a fallback row is a degraded reason, "calendar unverified", not a skipped brief, so the token line still ships daily), wait up to 90 seconds for live feed or memory-pull runs so a 07:03 brief does not read positions mid-upsert or memory files mid-pull, then run, save, and deliver; whatever raises inside the run, the job finishes the row failed with the reason and delivers before re-raising. The email goes out whatever happened: `Brief` (app/models/brief.rb) frames the model's text with the token line, the degraded reasons, per-feed as-of stamps, the fixed sentence "Earnings and economic calendar: not available in Phase 1" on the morning brief, the end-of-day sections from the database (fills, journal rows touched, missing post-mortems with links, the day's snapshot or "not yet written; equity as of HH:MM"), template-generated links only, and a footer with turns, calls, tokens, cost, and the stop reason; a failed run says "The brief is unavailable: <error>". The model's text is rendered as plain text and, in the HTML part, escaped before `simple_format`, so a planted link or tag never renders. Subjects carry a `DEGRADED`, `CAPPED`, or `FAILED` prefix and no dollar figures. Recipients are every user (both seeded users get every brief, as with the token nag). `emailed_at` is stamped after delivery; `bin/rails agent:resend[ID]` re-sends a stored run without another API call, and `agent:brief[kind]` runs one now. `MEMORY_REPO_PATH` is read into `config.x.memory.repo_path`; `Memory::Repo` reads the six known files only, 32 KB per file and 128 KB total, scrubbed to UTF-8, snapshotted once per run. `ANTHROPIC_ADAPTER=fake` (the default outside production) swaps in `Agent::FakeAnthropic`, a scripted stand-in that returns canned `Anthropic::Models::Message` objects.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:126:IRA and other read-only accounts sync positions and activities (for Phase 2 wash-sale checks) but never create trades. `Journal::BootstrapJob` and `Journal::LinkFillsJob` share one Solid Queue concurrency key per account, and every activity is linked in its own transaction: insert or find the leg by `activity_id`, recompute the trade as a pure function of its legs, then stamp `journaled_at` last. A rerun finds the existing leg and recomputes; it never skips because the activity was marked.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:158:- Oversized: remaining stop quantity above the position quantity. Schwab will reject the whole order at activation in a cash account, so this is flagged unprotected with its own reason (PRD §5.4.2 hard-alert case).
docs/phase-1-plan.md:167:For each trading-enabled account and each market day with a Schwab-sourced calendar row:
docs/phase-1-plan.md:182:**Model prerequisites built in this step.** `SchwabToken.status` (missing, revoked, expired, expiring, healthy; the Monday/Tuesday missed-Sunday nag stays in the health job) extracted from the health job and used by the banner, the system page, the mailer, and later `get_account_health`; `Account#sync_interval(feed)` and `Account#last_sync(feed)` extracted from the tick so views and scheduler cannot disagree; `trades.review_deferred_at` so Skip is durable ("later, not never": queue ordered by priority, then deferred-at nulls first, then oldest); `Position#distance_to_stop` guarded on a stop price rather than full protection so a partially covered stop still shows its distance; CASH_EQUIVALENT positions skipped by the positions sync so the sweep fund is neither a position nor a trade; `trading_enabled` requires `taxable`; an `account_configured` audit kind and an immediate sync enqueue when an account is enabled; setups creatable by name from the review form.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:186:| Dashboard | `/` | Banner by state: not connected (connect CTA); revoked (re-auth, with the refresh error); expired (re-auth, "sync paused since" from the first skipped run); expiring (re-auth, "Sunday's email will remind you" when covered); halted ("Halted by John, Mon 11:14a: reason. Clear it on the System page", no fake rule copy, re-auth link still shown if sync is also down); sync failed with a usable token (one-line status under the header, not a banner); needs setup (link to the accounts section). Equity with flow-adjusted change since the prior snapshot by date ("since Fri close" on a Monday, "first snapshot after today's close" before one exists); settled and unsettled cash ("unavailable", never zero, when both readings are nil); open risk labelled as known protected risk plus "N excluded: partial / oversized / awaiting / trailing / no stop"; positions as a list of rows sorted unprotected, awaiting, and trailing first then ascending distance, each with symbol, size, distance to stop as the primary number, the protection state inline, unrealized P&L (market value minus cost basis) second, and the older of the positions and orders as-of stamps; "N trades need review" callout; "Proposals: Phase 2" without a link; empty states for not connected, waiting for the first sync, and no open positions. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:189:| System | `/system` | Token status and age against 7 days with next Sunday 18:00 marked and a loud line when the token dies before it; last success and latest status per feed per account, `gap_from` when set, pause reason and since; failed sync runs plus Solid Queue failed executions in 7 days; shadow mode as a read-only indicator; API spend rendered as "0 runs, briefs start in step 9"; memory repo "never"; accounts table showing each account's equity and top symbols with an inline form for nickname, type, and enabled. Halt: set requires a reason and audits `manual_halt_set`; clear requires typed confirmation checked server-side and audits `manual_halt_cleared`. One word everywhere: Halt. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:190:| Re-auth | `/schwab/authorize` → Schwab → `/schwab/callback` | Built in step 4 |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:204:- **VCR cassettes** in `spec/cassettes`, recorded against Schwab only once the app is Ready For Use, with a scrubber that replaces bearer tokens, refresh tokens, and account hash values before write. Until then, cassettes are hand-built from community-client response shapes and marked `# SCHWAB_SHAPE_UNVERIFIED`.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:205:- **`Schwab::FakeClient`** implements the same interface as `Schwab::Client`, returns canned payloads, selected by `config.x.schwab.adapter`. Used by dev seeds and Playwright.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:206:- **Playwright** (`e2e/`, `@playwright/test` via npm, the only Node dependency): a scenarios route drawn only under `Rails.env.test?` and guarded again in the controller with `Schwab.fake?`, which resets the fake client, seeds with the factories (never by driving jobs), and signs in a seeded user by setting the session cookie because there is no inbox to read a magic link from. Times are relative: fresh is now, stale is three hours ago, expiring is 22 hours out, expired is two days ago. Scenarios: healthy, expiring, expired, halted, not connected, needs setup, one unprotected position, a failed sync with a usable token, the review queue with Skip persisting across reload, the post-mortem save, and re-auth success and failure (the seeded hash must match the fake's). The MCP endpoint is covered by request specs, not Playwright. The Playwright server runs with the test queue adapter.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:211:## 9. Deploy
docs/phase-1-plan.md:213:- `Procfile`: `web: bundle exec puma -C config/puma.rb`, `worker: bin/jobs`, `release: bin/rails db:prepare`. One Postgres 17 service and one database: Solid Queue, Solid Cache, and Solid Cable tables live in the primary schema (migration `InstallSolidTables`), so `db:prepare` on `DATABASE_URL` is the whole database setup.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:214:- `app.json` for the release phase.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:215:- `bin/dokku-setup`: documents postgres plugin, letsencrypt, config vars (`RAILS_MASTER_KEY`, `SCHWAB_CALLBACK_URL`, `MEMORY_REPO_PATH`), the storage mount for the memory repo (a clone on the host owned by uid 1000 so the container's `git pull --ff-only` can write `.git`; the runtime image carries git; if the remote needs a deploy key, a host cron doing the pull is the alternative and the job then only records what it finds), `ps:scale worker=1`, and the daily offsite DB dump from PRD §5.9 (postgres plugin backup to object storage on a cron).
docs/phase-1-plan.md:216:- `nginx.conf.d/mcp.conf` (or `dokku nginx:set client-max-body-size`): a small `client_max_body_size` (1 MiB) so the MCP body cap does not depend on Rails having already parsed the JSON for its log line.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:217:- App time zone `Eastern Time (US & Canada)`; Solid Queue recurring schedules use the same.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:221:## 10. Build order (green `bin/ci` at each step)
docs/phase-1-plan.md:225:3. Schwab adapter: `Schwab::Client` (OAuth, accounts, positions, orders, transactions, market hours, quotes, price history) with retry/backoff/limiter, `Schwab::FakeClient`, response mappers, `docs/verify-on-approval.md` started from §12.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:231:9. Scheduled runtime + mailers (briefs, nags, failure digest) + memory repo pull.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:232:10. Seeds for dev via FakeClient; `Procfile`, `app.json`, `bin/dokku-setup`; DHH-skill review pass.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:238:Guardrail engine, order proposals (tables only), approval UI, submission, brackets, reconciliation job, allocation module, FMP, earnings/econ calendar, watchlist screen (watchlist.md in the memory repo is read as text), push notifications, iOS shell.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:242:## 12. Schwab assumptions to verify on approval

